 ## Overview
-Provides fundamental linear algebra operations for 3x3 and 4x4 matrices, including norms, transposition, and multiplication variants [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3]. It serves as the computational core for [Custom Matrix Math Library](3216-custom-matrix-math-library.md), enabling the construction of view matrices and ray generation logic without external dependencies [G]. It distinguishes itself by offering explicit, unoptimized implementations for small fixed-size matrices, prioritizing clarity and correctness over performance [G].
+Provides fundamental arithmetic operations for 3x3 and 4x4 matrices and vectors, including norm calculations, transposition, and multiplication variants. These utilities support the linear algebra requirements of volume rendering camera models and transformation pipelines [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike high-level graphics libraries, this implementation focuses on explicit, low-level operations suitable for custom math libraries [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 
-**Inputs and Outputs**
-Inputs are 3x3 or 4x4 matrices and 3D/4D vectors stored as arrays of floating-point numbers [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3]. Outputs are scalar norms, transformed matrices, or transformed vectors [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3].
+### Data Structures
 
-**Data Structures**
-`Mat3` stores a general 3x3 matrix as 9 floats in row-major order: `m00, m01, m02, m10, m11, m12, m20, m21, m22` [S1, unofficial]. `SMat3` stores a symmetric 3x3 matrix as 6 floats representing the upper triangle: `m00, m01, m02, m11, m12, m22`, with implicit symmetry `m10 = m01`, `m20 = m02`, `m21 = m12` [S1, unofficial].
+**Mat3 (3x3 Matrix)**
+A structure containing 9 floating-point values in row-major order [S1, unofficial]:
+- Fields: `m00, m01, m02, m10, m11, m12, m20, m21, m22`
+- Layout: `[m00, m01, m02, m10, m11, m12, m20, m21, m22]` [S1, unofficial]
 
-**Numbered Steps**
-1. Initialize `Mat3` or `SMat3` structures with input values [S1, unofficial].
-2. Compute norms by summing squared elements and applying a square root [S1, unofficial].
-3. Compute matrix products by iterating row and column indices to calculate dot products [S1, unofficial].
-4. Compute $A^T A$ by mapping the result directly to an `SMat3` structure to exploit symmetry [S1, unofficial].
+**SMat3 (Symmetric 3x3 Matrix)**
+A structure storing only the upper triangle of a symmetric matrix, assuming $m_{ij} = m_{ji}$ [S1, unofficial]:
+- Fields: `m00, m01, m02, m11, m12, m22`
+- Layout: `[m00, m01, m02, m11, m12, m22]` [S1, unofficial]
+- Initialization: `setSymmetric(m00, m01, m02, m11, m12, m22)` sets all 6 fields [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Formulas**
-Frobenius norm for a general `Mat3` $A$:
-$$ ||A||_F = \sqrt{\sum_{i=0}^{2}\sum_{j=0}^{2} a_{ij}^2} = \sqrt{a_{00}^2 + a_{01}^2 + a_{02}^2 + a_{10}^2 + a_{11}^2 + a_{12}^2 + a_{20}^2 + a_{21}^2 + a_{22}^2} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+### Norms
 
-Frobenius norm for a symmetric `SMat3` $A$:
-$$ ||A||_F = \sqrt{a_{00}^2 + 2a_{01}^2 + 2a_{02}^2 + a_{11}^2 + 2a_{12}^2 + a_{22}^2} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+**Frobenius Norm (3x3)**
+Calculates the square root of the sum of squared elements [S1, unofficial]:
+$$||A||_F = \sqrt{\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}^2}$$
+Implementation sums all 9 squared elements of `Mat3` [S1, unofficial].
 
-Off-diagonal norm for a general `Mat3` $A$:
-$$ \text{off}(A) = \sqrt{a_{01}^2 + a_{02}^2 + a_{10}^2 + a_{12}^2 + a_{20}^2 + a_{21}^2} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+**Frobenius Norm (Symmetric 3x3)**
+Optimized for `SMat3` by accounting for symmetry ($a_{ij} = a_{ji}$) [S1, unofficial]:
+$$||A||_F = \sqrt{a_{00}^2 + 2a_{01}^2 + 2a_{02}^2 + a_{11}^2 + 2a_{12}^2 + a_{22}^2}$$
+Implementation uses the 6 stored coefficients with appropriate multipliers [S1, unofficial].
 
-Off-diagonal norm for a symmetric `SMat3` $A$:
-$$ \text{off}(A) = \sqrt{2(a_{01}^2 + a_{02}^2 + a_{12}^2)} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+**Off-Diagonal Norm (3x3)**
+Measures the magnitude of non-diagonal elements [S1, unofficial]:
+$$\text{off}(A) = \sqrt{a_{01}^2 + a_{02}^2 + a_{10}^2 + a_{12}^2 + a_{20}^2 + a_{21}^2}$$
+Implementation sums the squares of the 6 off-diagonal elements [S1, unofficial].
 
-3x3 matrix multiplication $C = AB$:
-$$ c_{ij} = \sum_{k=0}^{2} a_{ik} b_{kj} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+**Off-Diagonal Norm (Symmetric 3x3)**
+Optimized for `SMat3` [S1, unofficial]:
+$$\text{off}(A) = \sqrt{2(a_{01}^2 + a_{02}^2 + a_{12}^2)}$$
+Implementation uses the 3 stored off-diagonal coefficients with a multiplier of 2 [S1, unofficial].
 
-Compute $A^T A$ for symmetric output `Out`:
-$$ \text{Out}.m_{00} = a_{00}^2 + a_{10}^2 + a_{20}^2, \quad \text{Out}.m_{01} = a_{00}a_{01} + a_{10}a_{11} + a_{20}a_{21}, \quad \text{Out}.m_{02} = a_{00}a_{02} + a_{10}a_{12} + a_{20}a_{22} $$ [S1, unofficial]
-$$ \text{Out}.m_{11} = a_{01}^2 + a_{11}^2 + a_{21}^2, \quad \text{Out}.m_{12} = a_{01}a_{02} + a_{11}a_{12} + a_{21}a_{22}, \quad \text{Out}.m_{22} = a_{02}^2 + a_{12}^2 + a_{22}^2 $$ [S1, unofficial]
+### Matrix Multiplication
 
-**Parameters**
-Matrix elements $a_{ij}$ and $b_{ij}$ are 32-bit or 64-bit floating-point numbers [S1, unofficial][S3]. Vector components $xyz$ are 32-bit or 64-bit floating-point numbers [S3].
+**3x3 Matrix Multiplication**
+Computes $C = AB$ for two 3x3 matrices [S1, unofficial]:
+$$c_{ij} = \sum_{k=0}^2 a_{ik} b_{kj}$$
+Implemented explicitly for 3x3 matrices [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-Operations assume fixed-size matrices and vectors [G]. Precision loss in squared sums is not addressed in the sources [G]. Boundary conditions for degenerate or zero matrices are not specified [G].
+**Compute $A^T A$**
+Computes the product of a matrix transpose and the matrix itself, storing the result in a symmetric structure `Out` [S1, unofficial]:
+- `Out.m00 = a.m00^2 + a.m10^2 + a.m20^2`
+- `Out.m01 = a.m00*a.m01 + a.m10*a.m11 + a.m20*a.m21`
+- `Out.m02 = a.m00*a.m02 + a.m10*a.m12 + a.m20*a.m22`
+- `Out.m11 = a.m01^2 + a.m11^2 + a.m21^2`
+- `Out.m12 = a.m01*a.m02 + a.m11*a.m12 + a.m21*a.m22`
+- `Out.m22 = a.m02^2 + a.m12^2 + a.m22^2`
+This exploits symmetry to reduce computation and storage [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Pseudocode**
+**Vector Magnitude**
+Computes the Euclidean norm of a 4D vector [S2, unofficial]:
+$$\|u\| = \sqrt{\sum_{i=0}^{3} u_i^2}$$
+Implementation: `Math.sqrt(this.dot(this))` [S2, unofficial].
